diff --git a/docs/legion/bounded-retry-policy.md b/docs/legion/bounded-retry-policy.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f109b3556f0986a3aef44ac3b24d6b90a5129e28
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/legion/bounded-retry-policy.md
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# Bounded transport attempts
+
+`agent.api_max_retries` counts attempts in the ordinary API cycle. It does not include
+the separate primary-client rebuild cycle, fallback chain, or post-exhaustion recovery
+cycles. Zero is normalized to one ordinary attempt; it is not a global no-retry switch.
+The primary SDK's own transient retries are disabled so Hermes owns this policy.
+
+For a bounded acceptance run that must not repeat a failed transport request, use:
+
+```yaml
+agent:
+  api_max_retries: 1
+  primary_transport_recovery: false
+  auto_recovery_cycles: 0
+fallback_providers: []
+kanban:
+  turn_recovery_attempts: 0
+```
+
+`primary_transport_recovery` defaults to `true`, preserving the existing one-time client
+rebuild for transient transport failures. Set it to `false` per profile to skip that
+extra cycle and its wait. Other explicit recovery policies, such as format repair and
+credential refresh after a rejected request, retain their own contracts; this setting
+does not promise that every kind of provider failure has only one physical attempt.
+
+Kanban has another recovery budget: it can continue a failed turn in the same worker and
+session. `kanban.turn_recovery_attempts` defaults to three; zero disables those continuations.
+The legacy `HERMES_KANBAN_TURN_RECOVERY` environment override still takes precedence.
+Task-level `max_retries` controls new task attempts, not these in-place continuations.
+
+Existing agents keep their captured configuration. Change settings for future runs and
+load code updates only when the owning runtime is idle. No quota, credential, provider,
+fallback, review, or emergency-stop gate is changed by this option.
+
+Offline acceptance drops a local HTTP connection on the first request and offers a valid
+reply on the second. A real native Gateway and managed worker must make one request when
+recovery is disabled and two when enabled. This demonstrates transport-attempt behavior,
+not external provider availability or measured production token savings.
